# Vendored as a BASE, not verbatim (ruling T12-L). This is jvoisin/
# snuffleupagus's own config/default.rules for the v0.14.0 tag
# (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jvoisin/snuffleupagus/v0.14.0/config/default.rules,
# sha256 b465bab89a6d3cca0052d36fa031746f5c6e55a7a6298107c4bdd2503a2ea63c),
# with two kinds of departure from that tag, both narrow and both listed
# in full in the DIVERGENCES section directly below:
#   (a) local corrections to demonstrable defects found by actually
#       booting this file against real PHP, real command-injection shapes
#       and real Laravel/WordPress/PrestaShop source -- not this project
#       choosing to disagree with upstream's design, but fixing bytes or
#       patterns that don't do what the file's own intent (and, in one
#       case, upstream's own documented behavior) says they should; and
#   (b) the project-specific additions ("PROJECT EXEMPTIONS" and "our
#       addition" below), each independently justified and demonstrated
#       in both directions.
# The alternative to (a) -- shipping a rule verbatim once it's been
# demonstrated broken -- was tried and rejected over five review rounds:
# it means shipping rules that break the three applications this image
# exists to serve, which is the exact failure this whole task is about.
# Do not hand-edit anything in this file outside of extending the
# DIVERGENCES list itself with the same rigor (a live repro of the defect,
# a demonstrated fix in both directions, before/after evidence); re-fetch
# and re-verify future tags, then walk this list top to bottom to see
# whether each entry still applies.
#
# Re-sync on every snuffleupagus bump. The block between BEGIN upstream and
# END upstream must be the config/default.rules of the tag php/ext.json
# builds, with only the divergences below re-applied: diff the old tag's
# file against the new one, carry upstream's changes into all four
# rulesets, retire any divergence upstream has since fixed, and update the
# tag, URL and sha256 above. Running a newer module against an older
# ruleset is how 8.0-8.2 shipped mail() unguarded while v0.14.0's own file
# already had the fix. scripts/test_ext_registry.py fails when this tag
# and the built one differ.
#
# ============ DIVERGENCES FROM UPSTREAM v0.14.0 (ruling T12-L) =============
#
# 1. ASCII fix, one character (found during T12-C, the original vendoring).
#    Upstream's own comment on the open_basedir rule (originally:
#    "chdir('..');<U+2026 HORIZONTAL ELLIPSIS>;chdir('..')") contains one
#    non-ASCII character. The docs are explicit about the consequence:
#    "Rules, including comments, needs to be written in ASCII, other
#    encodings aren't supported and might cause syntax errors and related
#    issues like making all rules after non-ASCII symbols not considered
#    for execution and silently discarded" (doc/source/config.rst). That
#    is exactly what happened, confirmed by bisection: with the ellipsis
#    intact, every rule *after* that comment -- the entire "Prevent
#    system-related injections" block (system/exec/shell_exec/proc_open,
#    the headline protection this ruleset exists for), the full
#    include/require coverage, the backdoor-recon block, the curl/upload/
#    logging hardening, the webshell-pattern block -- silently stopped
#    registering. No parse error, no [config] warning, nothing in
#    `docker logs`. Verified both ways: reproduced the silent gap with the
#    ellipsis in place, confirmed every rule downstream of it works the
#    moment the ellipsis is replaced with three ASCII periods and nothing
#    else changes. Applied inline, at the `open_basedir` comment, below.
#
# 2. move_uploaded_file() destination patterns anchored to the actual file
#    extension (T12-J, round 6). Upstream's own patterns are `\.ph` /
#    `\.ht`, unanchored -- they match those letters *anywhere* in the
#    destination path, not just as the real extension. Demonstrated live
#    (round-2 re-review): `holiday.photo.jpg`, `my.phone.png`,
#    `chart.ht.svg` and `summer.photos.zip` all fatal move_uploaded_file()
#    under the unanchored rule, purely because "pho"/"ht" appears inside
#    an ordinary word -- and WordPress's own `wp_handle_upload()` puts the
#    sanitized *user* filename directly into this argument, so any media
#    upload whose name happens to contain those letters in sequence broke.
#    Fixed by anchoring to `$` (the real end of the path) with the
#    character class after "ph"/"ht" widened to any run of letters/digits,
#    so upstream's original breadth (catch .php, .php3, .phtml, .phar,
#    .pht, .htaccess, .htpasswd, ...) still holds, case-folded explicitly
#    since upload filenames aren't guaranteed lowercase.
#
#    The anchor itself was then found (same round, second pass) to trade
#    the false-positive bug for a narrower coverage regression: the
#    unanchored original blocked `shell.php ` (trailing space), `shell.
#    php.` (trailing dot) and similar junk-suffixed names by accident, of
#    matching "ph"/"ht" anywhere; a strict `$` anchor stopped blocking
#    them, since the trailing byte is no longer part of the letter/digit
#    run. Practical severity is low on this stack -- nginx's own `\.php$`
#    routing has the identical limitation, so a file actually named that
#    way is not straightforwardly served as PHP either -- but it is a real
#    regression from this file's own prior behavior, not a hypothetical
#    one. Closed by widening the trailing side of the anchor to also
#    absorb a run of spaces and/or dots before end-of-string (`[ .]*$`
#    instead of a bare `$`), so `shell.php`, `shell.php ` (one or more
#    trailing spaces), `shell.php.` / `shell.php...` (one or more trailing
#    dots) and any mixed run of the two all still match, while the four
#    legitimate names above -- none of which end in a run of bare spaces
#    or dots after their real extension -- are unaffected: the widened
#    tail only matters once the letter/digit run has already stopped at
#    the position that would otherwise be the true end of the path, so it
#    cannot reach back into an earlier "ph"/"ht" occurrence the way the
#    original unanchored pattern did. A trailing tab was tried and
#    dropped: the config DSL's string-literal parser does not reliably
#    pass `\t` through to the regex engine as a tab escape (confirmed by
#    a live probe: a real 0x09 byte in the destination did not match a
#    `\t` written into the pattern), and shipping an escape that quietly
#    doesn't do what its own text claims is worse than not having it --
#    tab-suffixed filenames are not covered, and this paragraph says so
#    rather than the pattern implying otherwise.
#
#    Demonstrated in full: all four legitimate names save cleanly, plain
#    and trailing-junk-suffixed shell.php/.PHP/.phtml/.phar/.htaccess/
#    .htpasswd all still fatal, including multi-character and mixed
#    trailing runs (`shell.php   `, `shell.php...`, `shell.php. `) and the
#    double-extension case (`shell.php.php`, blocked on its own real `.php`
#    ending regardless of the earlier one). Applied inline, at the "File
#    upload" section, below.
#
# 3. move_uploaded_file()'s new_path rules gated >=70000, not upstream's
#    >=70400. PHP 7.2 and 7.3 name the argument new_path too (their own
#    arginfo: path, new_path), so under upstream's gate those two versions
#    got only the "destination" rules -- a name no PHP this image builds
#    has -- and moved an upload to shell.php without a word. 7.4 and 8.x
#    already matched >=70400 and see no change.
#
# 4. Parameter-name gates of two vendored blocks corrected to PHP's real
#    arginfo (overturns the T12-K parking; tests/apps finding 5).
#    move_uploaded_file() is (path, new_path) on 7.x and (from, to) on
#    8.x; ini_set() is (varname, newvalue) on 7.x and (option, value) on
#    8.x. Upstream gates the new_path upload rules and the "Logging
#    lockdown" varname rules >=70000, and adds a "destination" branch no
#    PHP version has ever used, so every move_uploaded_file() call (all
#    versions) and every ini_set('error_log' | 'error_reporting' |
#    'display_errors') call (8.x) raised [config] warnings for a parameter
#    that does not exist. Those are PHP warnings, not log noise: PrestaShop's
#    webservice turns any warning into an error response, and POST
#    /api/images/products/<id> returned 500 under this ruleset on 7.4 and
#    8.0. The new_path and varname branches are now gated < 80000 and the
#    "destination" branch is gone; the drops are unchanged and still cover
#    every version, each under its real argument name. Applied inline at
#    the "File upload" and "Logging lockdown" sections below.
# =========== END DIVERGENCES FROM UPSTREAM v0.14.0 (ruling T12-L) ==========
#
# Why this replaced an earlier hand-written file (round-1 review, ruling
# T12-C): the hand-written version got syntax right after correction but
# semantics wrong in ways that took a real review to surface -- most
# seriously, its own unserialize() rule blocked Laravel's normal queue-job
# round trip (`unserialize(serialize($job))`, an `O:` payload) while a
# one-level-nested POP chain (`a:1:{i:0;O:...}`) sailed straight through
# and fired `__wakeup`/`__destruct`. A rule that looks protective and
# manufactures false confidence is worse than no rule; upstream's own file
# is written and tracked by people who know the tool's semantics across
# PHP versions, which plausibility-checked hand-written rules cannot match.
# laravel.rules / wordpress.rules / prestashop.rules duplicate this whole
# file rather than pulling it in via @include: @include is not real
# snuffleupagus syntax (checked against v0.14.0's grammar,
# src/sp_config_scanner.re -- it recognizes @condition, @end_condition,
# @set/set, @log/@info, @warn/@warning, @err/@error, and nothing named
# include). If this file changes, the same edit has to land in the other
# three.
#
# ========= BEGIN project exemptions (T12-D / T12-F / T12-G / T12-I) =========
# Exemptions below are evaluated before "BEGIN upstream" so each wins by
# file position over the vendored block's own unconditional rule for the
# same function -- rules for the same function are evaluated top to bottom
# across the whole file and the first match wins (doc/source/config.rst),
# confirmed directly (an early .allow(), separated from a later
# unconditional .drop() by unrelated rules in between, still wins by
# position, not proximity).
#
# T12-G, the reason most of these exist: round-2 review booted this file
# against real Laravel/WordPress/PrestaShop source (not synthetic probes)
# and found the vendored rules broke all three frameworks on their very
# first request -- ini_set('display_errors', ...) in Laravel's own
# exception bootstrap, function_exists('proc_open') in Symfony Console
# (loaded by every `php artisan` and by WordPress/PrestaShop's own Symfony
# Console-based CLI tooling), and eval() in Twig/Smarty template
# compilation. Synthetic rule-shape tests cannot catch this class of bug --
# they only test what was thought of; a real, if degenerate, framework
# codepath is what actually exercises what upstream's rules collide with.
#
# T12-I, round-2 re-review: every param-named exemption below MUST be
# version-split the same way upstream splits its own param-named rules
# (@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000 / >= 80000). Snuffleupagus matches
# .param() by the argument's *name*, and PHP 8.0 renamed several of these
# (ini_set: varname -> option; putenv: setting -> assignment). This
# project's own ext.json ships snuffleupagus for php>=7.2, so a rule using
# only the 8.x name is silently inert on 7.2/7.3/7.4 -- confirmed live
# against a 7.0-era build: a `.param("varname")`-only exemption never
# matches on 8.5, and (the bug this section fixes) a `.param("option")`-
# only exemption never matches on 7.x either, while the vendored *drop*
# rules for the same key -- which upstream did split -- still fire on
# whichever version the exemption missed. Net effect before this fix: the
# T12-G Critical was invisible on 8.5 (what every build and test in this
# task ran against) and still fatal on 7.2-7.4 (what this image also
# ships). Every exemption below now mirrors upstream's own split exactly.
#
# 1. putenv('DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH') -- Composer's Platform::clearEnv() calls
#    this as part of normal git version-guessing
#    (composer/src/Composer/Util/Git.php, "clean up env for OSX") --
#    Symfony components do the same defensive clear, so this is not
#    Composer-specific. Upstream's putenv hardening blocks any assignment
#    containing the *substring* "LD_", to stop LD_PRELOAD/LD_LIBRARY_PATH
#    injection; "DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH" contains that substring but is
#    macOS-only and inert on this (Linux) image -- there is nothing here
#    for it to poison. This is a genuine upstream over-match for this
#    platform: the rule's own justification (stop a real vector) does not
#    apply to this value, so blocking it protects nothing while
#    false-positiving any code that defensively clears it. Exempted by the
#    exact value, not the pattern: LD_PRELOAD, LD_LIBRARY_PATH and every
#    other real LD_-prefixed vector still matches the substring rule and
#    stays blocked (confirmed both directions; also confirmed the test that
#    checks this has teeth: loosened the exact-value match to a catch-all
#    in a mounted copy, watched LD_PRELOAD get wrongly allowed, restored).
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("setting").value("DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH").allow();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("assignment").value("DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH").allow();
@end_condition;

# 2. ini_set('display_errors' | 'memory_limit', ...) -- both confirmed by
#    reading the actual framework source, not inferred: Laravel's
#    HandleExceptions.php calls ini_set('display_errors', 'Off') on every
#    non-testing request; WordPress's wp_debug_mode() (wp-includes/load.php,
#    runs on every bootstrap) and its wp_raise_memory_limit() (called from
#    every wp-admin page, wp-admin/admin.php) call ini_set() on
#    display_errors and memory_limit respectively; PrestaShop's
#    config/defines.inc.php calls ini_set('display_errors', ...) on every
#    request depending on _PS_MODE_DEV_, and its SymfonyCacheClearer admin
#    operation calls ini_set('memory_limit', '-1').
#
#    (A third key, max_execution_time, was exempted here in round 3 on the
#    strength of a real PrestaShop call site -- but round-2 re-review found
#    nothing in the *vendored* file ever blocks that key at all: upstream's
#    complete ini_set drop list is assert.active/zend.assertions/
#    memory_limit/include_path/open_basedir plus error_log/error_reporting/
#    display_errors, and max_execution_time is in neither list. That
#    exemption was a no-op with a comment implying otherwise -- exactly the
#    false-reassurance shape this task has been eliminating all along.
#    Removed rather than left as harmless dead weight.)
#
#    These are full-key exemptions (any value), not value-scoped the way
#    the putenv fix above is, for a mechanical reason worth recording:
#    snuffleupagus's rule grammar allows at most one param/pos selector and
#    at most one value/value_r matcher per rule -- confirmed empirically,
#    three ways ("duplicate keyword 'param'" when a second .param() was
#    added; "duplicate keyword 'value_r'" when a second value matcher was
#    added even via a different selector; "'.value' and '.value_r' are
#    mutually exclusive" mixing the two forms) -- and upstream says the same
#    thing about itself, of a different key: "Since we have no way of
#    matching on two parameters at the same time, we're blocking calls to
#    open_basedir altogether" (this file's own open_basedir comment,
#    below). There is no way to write a single rule matching "option ==
#    display_errors AND value == off" -- only one of the call's two
#    arguments can be constrained per rule, so a rule scoped to the option
#    name necessarily allows every value for it.
#
#    For display_errors this does mean the ON direction -- the actual
#    information-disclosure risk the vendored rule exists to prevent -- is
#    no longer blocked either (confirmed: ini_set("display_errors","1")
#    now succeeds). Judged acceptable here: the realistic path to
#    *attacker-controlled* display_errors=on is a narrow options-injection
#    bug (user input reaching an ini_set() call directly), not general RCE
#    -- anyone who already has arbitrary code execution has far better
#    exfiltration paths than waiting for an error to print -- and the two
#    keys that would actually let an attacker redirect or silence logging
#    (error_log, error_reporting) are untouched by this exemption and stay
#    blocked.
#
#    For memory_limit, the DoS shape upstream's rule guards against (a
#    compromised request holding a worker open fatter than intended) is
#    NOT bounded the way an earlier draft of this comment claimed. A
#    container hitting its cgroup memory limit does not degrade one
#    request -- the kernel OOM-kills the whole container, taking every
#    FPM worker with it, which is a worse outcome than the per-request
#    fatal this rule used to produce, not a safety net under it. The
#    exemption is still the right call (WordPress's own admin dashboard
#    needs it on every page load, and the realistic path to an
#    *attacker-controlled* memory_limit value has the same narrow
#    options-injection precondition as display_errors above), but the
#    justification is "this protects little on its own merits" plus "a
#    real framework needs it constantly," not "something else catches the
#    worst case" -- it does not.
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("display_errors").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("memory_limit").allow();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("display_errors").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("memory_limit").allow();
@end_condition;

# Not exempted, and worth stating plainly rather than leaving implicit:
# disable_functions, allow_url_include, extension_dir and auto_prepend_file
# have no ini_set rule anywhere in this file, upstream or otherwise, and
# need none -- confirmed live (ini_set("disable_functions", "") returns
# false and changes nothing). They are PHP_INI_SYSTEM/PHP_INI_PERDIR
# settings: PHP itself refuses to let ini_set() touch them at runtime,
# regardless of what any snuffleupagus rule says. The protection is real,
# but it comes from PHP's own ini scoping, not from this ruleset --
# attributing it to a rule here would leave someone reading this file
# thinking a rule guards them.

# 3. function_exists() / is_callable() -- removed outright, not narrowed:
#    introspection is not invocation. Upstream's "backdoor recon" block
#    treats any function_exists()/is_callable() check on a short list of
#    dangerous function names (eval, exec, system, shell_exec, proc_open,
#    passthru) as suspicious, but that is also exactly what well-written
#    defensive code does before conditionally using one of those functions
#    -- confirmed live: Symfony Console's Terminal class (loaded by every
#    `php artisan` command, and by WordPress/PrestaShop's own
#    Symfony-Console-based CLI tooling) calls
#    function_exists('proc_open')/is_callable('proc_open') before
#    attempting to shell out for terminal width. Blocking the capability
#    check does not add protection the capability block itself doesn't
#    already provide -- the actual system()/exec()/proc_open() calls stay
#    covered by the command-injection rules elsewhere in this file,
#    unaffected by this exemption.
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").allow();
#
# A `require` exemption for Composer's own phar bootstrap
# (`/usr/local/bin/composer`) was tried and reverted (ruling T12-F). It was
# real and narrow -- scoped to that one root-owned, unwritable calling file,
# not to the require target -- but its only justification was making
# Composer work, and it did not: getting past that one call reaches
# ini_set('display_errors') and function_exists('proc_open')/
# is_callable('proc_open') (both exempted above now, but for framework
# compatibility broadly, not for Composer's benefit specifically -- Symfony
# Console's Terminal class probes these before shelling out for terminal
# width; setting COLUMNS/LINES in the environment skips that probe
# entirely too, no exemption needed for that part), and finally proc_open()
# itself: Symfony Console's fallback runs `stty -a | grep columns`, and the
# pipe character matches the exact command-injection filter this ruleset
# exists to provide -- `proc_open("echo hi")` is allowed, `proc_open("stty
# -a | grep columns")` is not, and that distinction is the headline
# protection doing its job, not an incidental false positive like the
# putenv case above. Composer's real work (git operations, VCS drivers,
# plugin scripts) would almost certainly surface more of the same if
# reached. Ruling: this is a property to document, not a bug to engineer
# around -- rulesets serve runtime request handling; Composer is
# build/deploy-time tooling, and the intended pattern is build with
# `cli-builder` and no ruleset, serve with `fpm`/`cli` and one.
# `PHP_SNUFFLEUPAGUS` and Composer are not meant to run together; do not
# "fix" this by exempting proc_open's metacharacter class.

# 4. curl_setopt($h, CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER, true) -- round-2 re-review
#    found this dropped under wordpress.rules, which breaks every outbound
#    HTTP call WordPress's own core makes (WP_Http_Curl::request(), used by
#    the update checks that run on ordinary wp-admin page loads). Root
#    cause: upstream's own allow rules below (`.param("value").value("1")`
#    / `.value("2")`) match the *strings* "1"/"2", but WP_Http_Curl passes
#    the PHP *bool* true -- confirmed live, `true` does not match either
#    allow rule, so the call falls through to the `option == 64` drop.
#    That drop rule exists specifically so CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER can never
#    be turned off; the WordPress call is the secure direction (enabling
#    verification, which is also curl's own default), not the dangerous
#    one, so blocking it is pure false positive, not a narrowing that
#    trades away real protection. Fixed by adding "true" as a recognized
#    safe value alongside "1"/"2", ahead of the vendored drop, the same
#    allow-before-drop shape upstream already uses for this exact rule.
#    "false" is deliberately not added: confirmed directly that
#    curl_setopt($h, CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER, false) still fatals under this
#    exemption -- disabling verification is the one direction that must
#    stay blocked, and it does.
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("value").value_r("^(1|true)$").allow();

# 5. ini_get() -- removed outright, same reasoning as 3: reading a setting
#    is not changing it. Upstream's recon block drops ini_get() of
#    allow_url_fopen, open_basedir and suhosin.*, and each of those is an
#    ordinary capability check in the applications these rulesets serve
#    (tests/apps finding 4): PrestaShop 8's AdminController reads
#    allow_url_fopen on every back-office page, so the whole back office
#    fataled; proxy-manager-lts reads suhosin.executor.disable_eval before
#    generating a proxy; Guzzle reads allow_url_fopen to pick a handler;
#    WordPress's automatic updater and site health read open_basedir.
#    Changing them stays covered: open_basedir by the ini_set() drops
#    below, while allow_url_fopen and suhosin.* are INI_SYSTEM and cannot be
#    changed at runtime at all.
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").allow();

# 6. include of a non-.php path from framework files that do it by design
#    (tests/apps findings 7, 8 and 9). Scoped by the *calling* file
#    (.filename_r()), not by the include target, so an include anywhere else
#    still has to pass the extension allow-list below. Shared by all four
#    rulesets like the rest of this section; a path that is not in the app
#    being served never matches.
#    - Symfony's HtmlErrorRenderer inlines its own CSS/JS/SVG assets with
#      include; without this every error page Symfony renders (a 405 in
#      Laravel, any Symfony error page in PrestaShop) became a 500.
#    - Symfony's PHP-files cache (PhpFilesTrait in 3.4/4.4, PhpFilesAdapter
#      in 5+) includes its entries, which are written without an extension;
#      PrestaShop's back office fataled on its first cache read.
#    - laravel/serializable-closure rebuilds a closure by including its own
#      laravel-serializable-closure:// stream. `route:cache` stores closure
#      routes that way, so with a cached route table Laravel 11+'s /up
#      health route, and every other closure route or queued closure,
#      fataled.
sp.disable_function.function("include").filename_r("/(symfony/error-handler|Symfony/Component/ErrorHandler)/ErrorRenderer/HtmlErrorRenderer\\.php$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("include").filename_r("/Symfony/Component/Cache/Traits/PhpFilesTrait\\.php$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("include").filename_r("/symfony/cache/Adapter/PhpFilesAdapter\\.php$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("include").filename_r("/laravel/serializable-closure/src/Serializers/Native\\.php$").allow();

# 7. curl_setopt(CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYHOST | CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER, 0) from
#    WordPress core's own HTTP transport (tests/apps finding 3b). WordPress
#    turns verification off only when the caller passed sslverify=false,
#    and core does exactly that for its loopback requests to itself: the
#    site health checks (a 500 on /wp-admin/site-health.php and its REST
#    endpoint) and spawn_cron(), which runs on an ordinary page view
#    whenever an event is due, so with the stock WP-Cron setup a visitor's
#    request fataled. Scoped to that one calling file and those two options;
#    a direct curl_setopt() anywhere else still cannot turn verification
#    off. Code that asks WordPress for sslverify=false gets it, as it does
#    without this ruleset.
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").filename_r("/wp-includes/Requests/src/Transport/Curl\\.php$").param("option").value("81").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").filename_r("/wp-includes/Requests/src/Transport/Curl\\.php$").param("option").value("64").allow();

# 8. proc_open('stty -a | grep columns') from Symfony Console's Terminal
#    class -- that one command, byte for byte, from that one file. Symfony
#    up to 5.x measures the terminal this way whenever COLUMNS is unset, and
#    a web request gets there too: Laravel's Artisan::call(), PrestaShop 8's
#    back-office "clear cache" (which runs cache:clear in-process, so the
#    drop landed after the redirect and the cache silently never cleared).
#    The T12-F note above still holds -- the metacharacter filter is not
#    widened, any other piped command stays blocked; this is an exact value.
#    Owner ruling 2026-09-30: these images are public, so a ruleset has to
#    run the applications it is named for unmodified, without asking users
#    to set COLUMNS/LINES or patch vendor code. Symfony 6+ passes an argv
#    array (['stty', '-a']), which the string filter never matched.
sp.disable_function.function("proc_open").filename_r("/(symfony/console|Symfony/Component/Console)/Terminal\\.php$").param("command").value("stty -a | grep columns").allow();
# ========== END project exemptions (T12-D / T12-F / T12-G / T12-I) ==========
#
# ============================ BEGIN upstream ============================
# This is the default configuration file for Snuffleupagus (https://snuffleupagus.rtfd.io).
# It contains "reasonable" defaults that won't break your websites,
# and a lot of commented directives that you can enable if you want to 
# have a better protection.

# Harden the PRNG
sp.harden_random.enable();

# sp.xxe_protection.enable() is deliberately absent: it switches libxml's entity
# loader off from an INI handler at startup, which does not carry into requests,
# so LIBXML_NOENT still expands file:// entities with it on. On PHP 7 it also
# nops the application's own libxml_disable_entity_loader(true). It would claim
# protection it does not give.

# Global configuration variables
# sp.global.secret_key("YOU _DO_ NEED TO CHANGE THIS WITH SOME RANDOM CHARACTERS.");

# Globally activate strict mode
# https://www.php.net/manual/en/language.types.declarations.php#language.types.declarations.strict
# sp.global_strict.enable();

# Prevent unserialize-related exploits
# sp.unserialize_hmac.enable();

# Only allow execution of read-only files. This is a low-hanging fruit that you should enable.
# sp.readonly_exec.enable();

# PHP has a lot of wrappers, most of them aren't usually useful, you should
# only enable the ones you're using.
# sp.wrappers_whitelist.list("file,php,phar");

# Prevent sloppy comparisons.
# sp.sloppy_comparison.enable();

# Use SameSite on session cookie
# https://snuffleupagus.readthedocs.io/features.html#protection-against-cross-site-request-forgery
sp.cookie.name("PHPSESSID").samesite("lax");

# Note that an attacker with arbitrary PHP code execution
# can bypass some virtual-patching, by (as)using PHP feature.
# A clever example would be to declare a class with a __toString method.

# Harden the `chmod` function (0777 (oct = 511, 0666 = 438)
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("chmod").param("mode").value("438").drop();
    sp.disable_function.function("chmod").param("mode").value("511").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("chmod").param("permissions").value("438").drop();
    sp.disable_function.function("chmod").param("permissions").value("511").drop();
@end_condition;

# Prevent various `mail`-related vulnerabilities
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("mail").param("additional_parameters").value_r("\\-").drop();
    sp.disable_function.function("mb_send_mail").param("additional_parameters").value_r("\\-").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("mail").param("additional_params").value_r("\\-").drop();
    sp.disable_function.function("mb_send_mail").param("additional_params").value_r("\\-").drop();
@end_condition;

# Since it's now burned, me might as well mitigate it publicly
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("setting").value_r("LD_").drop();
    sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("setting").value("PATH").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("assignment").value_r("LD_").drop();
    sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("assignment").value("PATH").drop();
@end_condition;

# This one was burned in Nov 2019 - https://gist.github.com/LoadLow/90b60bd5535d6c3927bb24d5f9955b80
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("setting").value_r("GCONV_").drop()
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("putenv").param("assignment").value_r("GCONV_").drop()
@end_condition;

# https://github.com/php/php-src/issues/22035
# CURLOPT_SSLENGINE = 10089 
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("option").value("10089").drop()
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("option").value("10089").drop()
@end_condition;

# Since people are stupid enough to use `extract` on things like $_GET or $_POST, we might as well mitigate this vector
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("extract").pos("0").value_r("^_").drop()
sp.disable_function.function("extract").pos("1").value("0").drop()
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("extract").param("array").value_r("^_").drop()
sp.disable_function.function("extract").param("flags").value("0").drop()
@end_condition;

# This is also burned:
# ini_set('open_basedir','..');chdir('..');...;chdir('..');ini_set('open_basedir','/');echo(file_get_contents('/etc/passwd'));
# Since we have no way of matching on two parameters at the same time, we're
# blocking calls to open_basedir altogether: nobody is using it via ini_set anyway.
# Moreover, there are non-public bypasses that are also using this vector ;)
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value_r("open_basedir").drop()
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value_r("open_basedir").drop()
@end_condition;

# Prevent various `include`-related vulnerabilities
sp.disable_function.function("require_once").value_r("\.(inc|phtml|php)$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("include_once").value_r("\.(inc|phtml|php)$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("require").value_r("\.(inc|phtml|php)$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("include").value_r("\.(inc|phtml|php)$").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("require_once").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("include_once").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("require").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("include").drop();

# Prevent `system`-related injections
sp.disable_function.function("system").param("command").value_r("[$|;&`\\n\\(\\)\\\\]").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("shell_exec").pos("0").value_r("[$|;&`\\n\\(\\)\\\\]").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
    sp.disable_function.function("shell_exec").param("command").value_r("[$|;&`\\n\\(\\)\\\\]").drop();
@end_condition;
sp.disable_function.function("exec").param("command").value_r("[$|;&`\\n\\(\\)\\\\]").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("proc_open").param("command").value_r("[$|;&`\\n\\(\\)\\\\]").drop();

# Prevent runtime modification of interesting things
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("assert.active").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("zend.assertions").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("memory_limit").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("include_path").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value("open_basedir").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("assert.active").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("zend.assertions").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("memory_limit").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("include_path").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value("open_basedir").drop();
@end_condition;

# Detect some backdoors via environment recon
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").param("varname").value("allow_url_fopen").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").param("varname").value("open_basedir").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").param("varname").value_r("suhosin").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function_name").value("eval").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function_name").value("exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function_name").value("system").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function_name").value("shell_exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function_name").value("proc_open").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function_name").value("passthru").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("var").value("eval").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("var").value("exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("var").value("system").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("var").value("shell_exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("var").value("proc_open").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("var").value("passthru").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").param("option").value("allow_url_fopen").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").param("option").value("open_basedir").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_get").param("option").value_r("suhosin").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function").value("eval").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function").value("exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function").value("system").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function").value("shell_exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function").value("proc_open").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("function_exists").param("function").value("passthru").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("value").value("eval").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("value").value("exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("value").value("system").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("value").value("shell_exec").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("value").value("proc_open").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("is_callable").param("value").value("passthru").drop();
@end_condition;


# Ghetto error-based sqli detection
# sp.disable_function.function("mysql_query").ret("FALSE").drop();
# sp.disable_function.function("mysqli_query").ret("FALSE").drop();
# sp.disable_function.function("PDO::query").ret("FALSE").drop();

# Ensure that certificates are properly verified
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("value").value("1").allow();
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("value").value("2").allow();
# `81` is SSL_VERIFYHOST and `64` SSL_VERIFYPEER
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("option").value("64").drop().alias("Please don't turn CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYCLIENT off.");
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("option").value("81").drop().alias("Please don't turn CURLOPT_SSL_VERIFYHOST off.");

# Ensure that file:// protocol is not allowed in CURL
sp.disable_function.function("curl_setopt").param("value").value_r("file://").drop().alias("file:// protocol is disabled");
sp.disable_function.function("curl_init").param("url").value_r("file://").drop().alias("file:// protocol is disabled");

# File upload
#
# LOCAL CORRECTION (T12-J, see the divergence section near the top of this
# file): upstream's own patterns here are "\.ph" / "\.ht", unanchored --
# they match those two letters anywhere in the destination path, not just
# as the actual extension. Round-2 re-review demonstrated the false
# positives live: holiday.photo.jpg, my.phone.png, chart.ht.svg and
# summer.photos.zip all fatal move_uploaded_file() under this rule, purely
# because "pho"/"ht" appears inside an ordinary word -- and
# wp_handle_upload() puts the sanitized *user* filename directly into this
# argument, so any WordPress media upload whose name happens to contain
# those letters in sequence breaks. Anchored to end-of-path, with the
# character class after "ph"/"ht" widened from nothing to any run of
# letters/digits so the same breadth upstream intended (catch .php, .php3,
# .phtml, .phar, .pht, .htaccess, .htpasswd, ...) still holds -- just
# anchored to where an extension actually is instead of matching anywhere
# in the string. Case folded explicitly ([pP][hH], [hH][tT]) since upload
# filenames are not guaranteed lowercase.
#
# The anchor is `[ .]*$`, not a bare `$`: a strict end-of-string anchor
# stopped blocking `shell.php ` (trailing space) and `shell.php.`
# (trailing dot), which the old unanchored rule blocked by accident. Low
# practical severity here (nginx's own `\.php$` routing has the identical
# limitation), but a real regression from this file's own prior behavior,
# found and closed in the same round -- see the divergence section. Any
# run of trailing spaces and/or dots is absorbed before the end anchor;
# trailing tabs are not (the config DSL does not reliably pass `\t`
# through to the regex engine as a tab escape) and this is not claimed.
#
# Demonstrated both directions: all four legitimate filenames above still
# save cleanly; shell.php, shell.PHP, shell.phtml, shell.phar, .htaccess
# and x.htpasswd all still fatal, plain and with trailing spaces/dots
# (single, repeated and mixed) appended.
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("move_uploaded_file").param("to").value_r("\\.[pP][hH][a-zA-Z0-9]*[ .]*$").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("move_uploaded_file").param("to").value_r("\\.[hH][tT][a-zA-Z0-9]*[ .]*$").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("move_uploaded_file").param("new_path").value_r("\\.[pP][hH][a-zA-Z0-9]*[ .]*$").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("move_uploaded_file").param("new_path").value_r("\\.[hH][tT][a-zA-Z0-9]*[ .]*$").drop();
@end_condition;

# Logging lockdown
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID < 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value_r("error_log").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value_r("error_reporting").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("varname").value_r("display_errors").drop();
@condition PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80000;
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value_r("error_log").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value_r("error_reporting").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("ini_set").param("option").value_r("display_errors").drop();
@end_condition;

# Classic webshells patterns
sp.disable_function.function("system>base64_decode").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("shell_exec>base64_decode").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("exec>base64_decode").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("passthru>base64_decode").drop();
sp.disable_function.function("proc_open>base64_decode").drop();
sp.eval_blacklist.list("system,exec,shell_exec,passthru,proc_open");
# ============================= END upstream ==============================

# ============================ our addition ================================
# Reject uploads whose content is executable PHP, regardless of extension --
# not part of upstream's default.rules, this is the sp-upload-check script
# this project ships (rootfs/usr/local/bin/sp-upload-check). Fixed in the
# round-1 review (I-2, I-3): it now skips archives by magic byte (WordPress
# plugin installs and PrestaShop module installs upload zips by design, and
# a naive content-sniff rejected them depending on how zip happened to store
# their entries), catches the `<?=` short-echo tag alongside `<?php`, and
# fails closed (rejects) rather than open when it cannot read the file --
# see that script's own header for the demonstrated fix of each.
sp.upload_validation.script("/usr/local/bin/sp-upload-check").enable();
